/*
   ================================================================================
   ESP32 Fridge Monitor - Samsung SRS702GDHSS side-by-side  (v1.51)
   ================================================================================
   Board: ESP32-C3 Super Mini  (Arduino board: "ESP32C3 Dev Module")
   Tools menu: USB CDC On Boot = Enabled (for USB serial debug output)

   WHAT IT DOES:
     Listens to the fridge's GATEWAY port (blue connector on the main board),
     decodes its status messages, shows them on a web page and sends them to
     Home Assistant over MQTT (with auto-discovery). At a set interval it also
     asks the fridge for its status (the "keepalive"), which keeps the fridge
     sending data.

   WEB PAGES:
     Status        what the fridge is doing, uptime, and the recent changes
     Diagnostics   link information, captured data (hex / ASCII, downloadable)
     Serial Setup  baud rate, format, polarity, frame gap, and the keepalive
     Wi-Fi & MQTT  join a network, MQTT broker settings, forget Wi-Fi
     Settings      +/- adjustment of the fridge and freezer sensor temperatures, ice bucket delay

   THE FRIDGE PROTOCOL (9600 baud 8N1, 31-byte messages):
     byte 0..3   02 96 03 41 (header)
     byte 4      counter 0..15
     byte 5      message type: 07 = fridge status (sent when something changes,
                 and in repeated "poll bursts"), 02 = the fridge's answer to a
                 request, 05 = start-up / identity frame.
                 Requests the fridge answers: 00 and 01 (read status -> 02),
                 04 (read identity -> 05), 06 (write settings -> 02, beeps)
     byte 8      flags: 0x01 lighting, 0x02/0x04 dispenser selection, 0x08 Ice Off,
                 0x10 Child Lock, 0x20 Vacation, 0x80 Power Freeze
     byte 9      low nibble fridge set temperature index (7 - index = deg C),
                 high nibble freezer index (-(14 + index) deg C)
     byte 10     freezer temperature (top bit = minus), byte 11 fridge temperature
                 (in settings mode they show the setting instead)
     byte 12/13  raw freezer / fridge sensors (NTC thermistors, calibrated here)
     byte 16     loads: 0x80 compressor, 0x10/0x08 fridge fan, 0x40/0x20 freezer fan,
                 0x02/0x04 condenser fan
     byte 17     0x03 cooling mode, 0x04 ice dispensing, 0x20 gateway link,
                 0x40 cooling hold, 0x80 defrost heater
     byte 18     0x40 fridge door, 0x80 freezer door, 0x10 ice bucket full,
                 0x02/0x04 fridge/freezer settings mode
     byte 19     bit 0x04 toggles during poll bursts
     Not decoded yet (sent to Home Assistant as "Fridge Unknown ..."): bytes 6, 7,
     14, 15 (constant so far: 00 54 .. 14 3C), bytes 20-28 (always "000000000"),
     byte 8 bit 0x40, byte 16 bit 0x01, byte 17 bits 0x08/0x10,
     byte 18 bits 0x01/0x08/0x20, byte 19 every bit except 0x04
     byte 29     XOR of bytes 0..28, byte 30 = 00
     The fridge stops sending after a while unless something keeps asking it for
     its status. A type 00 read every 90 s fixed that (runs of 34 h and more).

   WHAT CHANGED IN v1.51:
     - The original "Fridge Ice Bucket Full" (raw on/off, as the fridge reports it)
       is back. The stabilised one is "Fridge Ice Bucket (Stabilised)", Full / Not full.
     - The ice bucket delay (default 4 min) is set on the Settings page (was the
       Calibration page, now /settings; /calibration still works).

   WHAT CHANGED IN v1.50:
     - Ice bucket: only reported Full once byte 18 bit 0x10 has been on for 4 minutes
       (ICE_FULL_MS). The ice maker's level checks set it for up to ~3 min, which
       showed as false "full" readings.
     - Home Assistant: "Fridge Ice Bucket" sensor showing Full / Not full replaces the
       on/off "Fridge Ice Bucket Full" binary sensor (removed from Home Assistant).

   WHAT CHANGED IN v1.49:
     - The rolling 24 h ice count carries on through restarts: the time of each
       batch is saved in flash (last 48 batches), not just kept in memory.
     - Fixed: after a restart the "last batch" time on the Status page showed the
       time since the restart. The last batch time is now saved too.

   WHAT CHANGED IN v1.48:
     - The ice batch total (and so "Fridge Ice Cubes Total") is saved in flash, so
       it keeps counting through restarts and power cuts instead of starting at 0.

   WHAT CHANGED IN v1.47:
     - Ice cubes: the tray makes 7 cubes per batch (ICE_CUBES_PER_FILL). New Home
       Assistant sensors "Fridge Ice Cubes (24 h)" (rolling 24 hours) and
       "Fridge Ice Cubes Total" (all time). The Status page shows batches and cubes.

   WHAT CHANGED IN v1.46:
     - Home Assistant: Fridge Lighting and Fridge Child Lock show plain On / Off.
       (Lighting was a "light" sensor, shown as Light detected / No light, and
       Child Lock was a "lock", which Home Assistant shows inverted: on = Unlocked.)

   WHAT CHANGED IN v1.45:
     - Diagnostics page: "Undecoded Bytes" table with the current value of every
       undecoded byte/bit and when it last changed, plus a log of the last 10
       undecoded changes with date and time.

   WHAT CHANGED IN v1.44:
     - Bytes and bits that are not decoded yet are sent to Home Assistant on
       <prefix>/unknown, as diagnostic entities on the Fridge Serial Monitor device:
         sensors        Fridge Unknown Byte 6, 7, 14, 15 and Bytes 20-28 (hex)
         binary sensors Fridge Unknown Byte 8 Bit 0x40, Byte 16 Bit 0x01,
                        Byte 17 Bits 0x08 / 0x10, Byte 18 Bits 0x01 / 0x08 / 0x20,
                        Byte 19 Bits 0x01 0x02 0x08 0x10 0x20 0x40 0x80
         sensor         Fridge Unknown Last Change (what moved, with a timestamp)
       Their history in Home Assistant shows when they change, to line up
       against what the fridge was doing. They are not shown on the web page.

   WHAT CHANGED IN v1.43:
     - Recent Changes: every entry starts with a short date, e.g. 28/10/26 1:15 am.

   WHAT CHANGED IN v1.42:
     - Recent Changes on a phone: shorter times (time only for today, "27 Oct"
       plus time for older entries) so they no longer run into the text.

   WHAT CHANGED IN v1.41:
     - Read-only: the type 06 set temperature test is removed. The only thing the
       ESP ever sends to the fridge is the keepalive status read (type 00).
       (The test showed the fridge beeps and acknowledges a type 06 write, but
       does not take the set temperature from byte 9.)
     - Web page fits a phone screen: the menu wraps instead of pushing the page
       sideways, and the margins are smaller on narrow screens.

   WHAT CHANGED IN v1.40:
     - Fixed: after the ESP restarted, the keepalive waited for the fridge to send
       a status frame before sending anything, which could take a long time (or
       never happen if the fridge had gone quiet). It now starts straight away
       with a built-in frame, and switches to the fridge's own frame once one
       has been seen.
     - The fridge's answers (type 02) now also fill the Status page after a
       restart, so it doesn't wait for a door opening or a poll burst.

   WHAT CHANGED IN v1.39 (a finished version of the 1.x test sketch):
     - Pages: Status, Diagnostics, Serial Setup, Wi-Fi & MQTT, Calibration.
     - Status page: no raw values or unknown bits, uptime added, run tracking
       removed. Recent Changes lists only real changes (doors, set temperatures,
       displayed temperatures, compressor, dispenser, defrost, ice maker...),
       not keepalives, poll bursts, fans or cooling mode.
     - Captured data moved to the Diagnostics page.
     - Keepalive is on by default (type 00 read, every 90 s), interval set in
       seconds on the Serial Setup page. The interval saved by v1.3x is kept.
     - New: type 06 set temperature command test (Diagnostics page).
     - Calibration: simple +/- adjustment for fridge and freezer.
     - Removed: test transmit, break, auto-probe, baud auto-detect, maintenance
       buttons, run tracking, and the raw/unknown Home Assistant entities
       (their old discovery entries are cleared when MQTT connects).
     - Temperatures stay live between poll bursts (read from the fridge's
       type 02 answers to the keepalive).

   LIBRARIES: WiFiManager (tzapu), PubSubClient, ArduinoJson (v7).

   WIRING:
     Through a bidirectional logic level converter (e.g. BSS138 board):
     - Converter LV -> 3.3V,  HV -> fridge logic voltage (MEASURE IT FIRST)
     - Converter GND (both sides) -> Board GND and Fridge GND
     - Fridge TX -> HV1 -> LV1 -> GPIO 20 (labelled "RX" / "20")
     - GPIO 7    -> LV2 -> HV2 -> Fridge RX
     - Do NOT use GPIO 21 (labelled "TX") for TX: the chip prints boot
       messages on it at every reset, which would go to the fridge.
     - SAFETY: many appliance control boards are NOT isolated from mains, so
       their "GND" can be live. Check before connecting, and do not have the
       ESP32 plugged into a PC by USB while it is wired to the fridge unless
       you know the port is isolated. Power it from the fridge's own low
       voltage rail or an isolated supply and use the web page / OTA.
   ================================================================================
*/

